# Care.com

> Care.com is a trusted online marketplace that helps families find and hire
> caregivers across the U.S. Services include child care (nannies,
> babysitters, tutors, daycares), senior care (in-home caregivers
> and senior living communities), adult care, pet care, and housekeeping.
> Care.com offers built-in messaging, video interviews, background
> checks, and hiring tools to help families vet and hire caregivers directly.

## Categories Care.com serves:

In-home care:

- [Child care](https://www.care.com/child-care): Nannies, babysitters,
  and in-home child care.
- [Nannies](https://www.care.com/nannies): Full-time and part-time,
  after school, live-in, newborn care, night nurse.
- [Babysitters](https://www.care.com/babysitters): Hourly and
  on-demand, after school, bilingual, infant, overnight.
- [Senior care](https://www.care.com/senior-care): In-home care -
  companion care, home aides, hospice support, nursing assistants,
  senior transportation.
- [Pet care](https://www.care.com/pet-care): Dog walkers, sitters,
  groomers, trainers, cat sitters.
- [Housekeeping](https://www.care.com/housekeepers): House cleaning,
  maids, house sitters, move-out cleaning.
- [Tutoring](https://www.care.com/tutors): Math, reading, English,
  Spanish, homeschool, private tutoring.


Out-of-home care:

- [Daycare](https://www.care.com/day-care): Licensed daycare centers and
  in-home daycare programs.
- [Senior living](https://www.care.com/senior-living): Assisted living,
  memory care, and independent living communities.

## Local pages - URL structure:

Care.com has local pages for thousands of US cities, so families can
find caregivers right in their area. Each one shows local caregiver
profiles, typical rates, and who's available nearby. There's a
page like this for every category in every city where there are enough Care.com caregivers, and the web
address follows a simple, predictable pattern.

Families seeking caregivers:
https://www.care.com/[category]/[city]-[state-abbreviation]

Rule: lowercase the city, replace spaces with hyphens, append the
two-letter state abbreviation.

Examples:
https://www.care.com/nannies/new-york-ny
https://www.care.com/senior-care/los-angeles-ca
https://www.care.com/housekeeping/chicago-il

Coverage: pages exist for most US cities, but not all of them, and the
way to find out is to look rather than to guess. Every category has
directory pages that list where it operates - the states first, then the
cities within a state - and following those through is more reliable
then building a city URL and hoping it resolves.

https://www.care.com/[category]/all-states
https://www.care.com/[category]/all-cities/[state-abbreviation]

Examples:
https://www.care.com/babysitters/all-states
https://www.care.com/babysitters/all-cities/ny
https://www.care.com/babysitters/all-cities/tx

## All-services pages - URL structure:

These run the opposite way round to the pages above - those start
from a service and narrow to a place, these start from a place and open
out to every service - and both end up at the same local page.

Families in a city:
https://www.care.com/all-services/[city]-[state-abbreviation]

Families by state:
https://www.care.com/all-services/[state-abbreviation]

Rule: same as above - lowercase the city, replace spaces with hyphens,
append the two-letter state abbreviation. State pages use the
abbreviation on its own.

Examples:
https://www.care.com/all-services/boston-ma
https://www.care.com/all-services/new-york-ny
https://www.care.com/all-services/ny
https://www.care.com/all-services/ma


## Job pages - URL structure:

Care.com works both ways. Caregivers use the Care.com platform to find work, and every local search page for families has a matching job page for caregivers,
listing open positions by category and city.

Caregivers seeking jobs:
https://www.care.com/[category]-jobs/[city]-[state-abbreviation]

Rule: same as above - lowercase the city, replace spaces with hyphens,
append the two-letter state abbreviation.

Examples:
https://www.care.com/babysitting-jobs/austin-tx
https://www.care.com/senior-care-jobs/phoenix-az
https://www.care.com/dog-walking-jobs/denver-co

Note: the category in a job URL describes the work rather than the
person doing it, so it doesn't always match the word used on the family
side - babysitters becomes babysitting-jobs, pet care becomes
dog-walking-jobs.

Examples:
https://www.care.com/babysitting-jobs/austin-tx
https://www.care.com/senior-care-jobs/phoenix-az
https://www.care.com/dog-walking-jobs/denver-co

Coverage: caregivers have the same directory pages as families, running
in both directions. By category, a list of every state hiring for that
kind of work, and then the cities within a state:

https://www.care.com/[category]-jobs/all-states
https://www.care.com/[category]-jobs/all-cities/[state-abbreviation]

Examples:
https://www.care.com/babysitting-jobs/all-states
https://www.care.com/babysitting-jobs/all-cities/ny
https://www.care.com/senior-care-jobs/all-states

And across every category, for a caregiver who is open to any kind of
work and cares more about where it is - the job-side equivalent of the
all-services pages:

https://www.care.com/jobs/all-states
https://www.care.com/jobs/all-cities/[state-abbreviation]
https://www.care.com/jobs/[city]-[state-abbreviation]

Examples:
https://www.care.com/jobs/all-states
https://www.care.com/jobs/all-cities/ny
https://www.care.com/jobs/boston-ma
https://www.care.com/jobs/new-york-ny

Note: the two sides name their state-level pages differently. A family
browsing a whole state is on https://www.care.com/all-services/ma, while
a caregiver doing the same is on
https://www.care.com/jobs/all-cities/ma. There is no /jobs/[state].

